dear pierre gattas and marcos baira  distinguished guests and dear friends  i'm so sorry i cannot welcome you to the  berlimo today  i saw some of you just a couple of weeks  ago and i saw others in evian at the end  of the summer  and our meetings are always a great  source of ideas and inspiration  i know that you have a very dense  program with my colleagues and my team  today  so i won't keep you too long  i would just like to introduce three  topics  that i am sure will feature prominently  in today's meetings  so  first a renewed focus on international  trade and connectivity is necessary  in the coming weeks we will present our  new connectivity strategy called global  gateway europe  we want to step up our investment in  different kinds of infrastructure  to connect the world  including for example digital  connections ranging from submarine  cables  to cloud infrastructure  but also energy connections such as new  hydrogen market with the african  continent  and in each of these fields  we will work to ensure  that european industry can operate  on an equal footing with foreign  competitors  think about this  18 of jobs in the european union are  linked to our  exports so global gateway europe is  immensely important for our recovery  but it goes much further  europe is offering transparency  and good governance to our partners  my second point  is a crucial topic  we need to focus on value chains  we all by now know well  that the european union is critically  dependent  on imports from threat countries  we don't want our production lines to be  slowed down by global shortages  and foreign bottlenecks  we want to work with you  to strengthen europe's resilience  the industry's feedback will be  essential  to assess  and to address these dependencies  on our side  we will continue to seek partnerships  and alliances with the private sector  this is the logic of our new industrial  alliances  and it is also the logic of the european  ships act  and we are glad to see that a giant like  intel  has just announced  an investment of up to 95 billion  dollars in european chip making  facilities  so together we can create a  state-of-the-art european ship ecosystem  that is at the same time  open to the world  and competitive on global markets  and my third point  i know that the current rise of energy  prices  is very high on your gender and i can  assure you it's high on our agenda as  well  the whole world is affected  irrespective of countries energy  and climate policies or electricity  market design  and we have to draw lessons from this  experience  and let me name three  my first is we are too dependent on gas  ninety percent of the gas we use is  imported and that is not sustainable  we need to discuss a european strategic  reserve  and we need to discuss the possibility  of common procurement  my second point is  we need to make our energy system more  resilient to price and supply shocks  thus investing in renewables is the  obvious policy choice  they are increasingly cheaper in  production  their production prices and costs have  decreased over the last years  and renewables are homegrown  alongside this  we need stable sources  nuclear  and during the transition  natural gas  and my third point  is that in the short term we need to  support those affected most by the  energy price shocks  and that is vulnerable consumers  and heavily exposed businesses  measures can be taken very swiftly  eu rules allow relief through state aid  they allow targeted support to consumers  and of course to cut energy taxes or  reduce levees on energy bills  but the long-term solution lies in the  european green deal  we must keep on investing in renewable  clean energy  and in europe's competitiveness on the  green markets of tomorrow  and this is exactly what we are doing  with next generation you our recovery  plan  in the national plans submitted so far  36 billion euros will be spent for clean  energy  i'm speaking of green hydrogen for  example or offshore and onshore wind  farms  or new industrial sites using renewable  energy  partnerships with the business sectors  are vital to this  and together  we will make europe more competitive and  resilient to price shocks  europe is stronger when governments and  businesses join forces towards common  goals  so thank you very much for listening to  me and giving me your attention  and let me wish you a very fruitful day  at the european commission
 

